Minor localized skin maceration and erythema occurring under a recently applied joint brace
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Brace-patterned erythema, Softened hyperhydrated epidermis, Musty odor upon removal

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-029
Reason for Visit: New onset of a painful, foul-smelling rash developing under an orthopedic brace, requiring assessment for complicated cellulitis or deep space infection.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a red, malodorous skin breakdown occurring beneath an occlusive orthopedic device. Urgent physical assessment was strictly required prior to the next in-hours period to unbind the device, evaluate the integrity of the dermal barrier, and rule out necrotizing soft tissue infection or evolving cellulitis before reapplying the brace.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: The skin under my knee brace is red, soggy, and smells terrible.
History of Present Illness: Patient describes patchy erythema restricted exactly to the skin beneath a recently applied orthopedic brace. Reports the skin has a softened white hyperhydrated appearance. Notes mild localized pruritus and a burning sensation, especially after physical exertion and trapped sweating. Reports a distinct foul or musty odor upon complete brace removal. Denies fevers or chills.
Functional Status: Ambulating with the brace, independent in ADLs.
Social/Safety Concerns: Orthopedic injury requires continued brace use, complicating the management of the skin breakdown.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 124/80 | HR: 78 | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 76 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing, focused on the skin condition beneath the brace.
Physical Exam: Removal of the brace reveals patchy erythema corresponding exactly to the brace pattern. The epidermis appears softened, white, and hyperhydrated. A distinct musty odor is noted upon removal. Diagnostic triad confirmed: brace-patterned erythema, softened hyperhydrated epidermis, and musty odor upon removal. Absence of distinct purulent fluid drainage, deep induration, or spreading cellulitis beyond the brace lines. Epidermal barrier is fragile but intact. Lack of systemic infectious markers.
Environmental Observations: Knee brace is damp with sweat.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current topical agents or oral antibiotics.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Minor localized skin maceration and erythema occurring under a recently applied joint brace - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Erythema is strictly geometric and confined to the occluded area, without deep warmth or induration extending beyond the brace.

Cutaneous Abscess - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of palpable fluctuance or purulent drainage.

Severe Contact Dermatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Skin changes characteristic of moisture maceration rather than allergic blistering, and presence of hyperhydrated white skin.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient developed acute skin breakdown beneath an occlusive device, mimicking early signs of a severe soft tissue infection. Given the presence of a foul odor and skin breakdown, a face-to-face home assessment was required to remove the device and directly inspect the tissue. Delaying assessment carried the risk of uncontrolled bacterial overgrowth in a compromised dermal environment. Confirming simple moisture maceration safely averted inappropriate antibiotic use and allowed for proper environmental modification.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Thorough cleansing of the macerated skin with mild soap and water, followed by complete air drying. Recommendation to wash and thoroughly dry the brace padding. Application of a thin layer of zinc oxide barrier cream.
Positive Behavioral Change: Wear a clean, breathable cotton sleeve (like a cut-off sock) underneath the brace, changing it twice daily to wick moisture away from the skin and prevent further maceration.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): Cotton tubigrip or protective sleeve.
Follow-up: Routine GP review if the skin breaks open entirely or becomes intensely painful.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if the redness spreads rapidly outside the brace area, if you develop a high fever and chills, or if the skin turns black or extremely painful.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical moisture maceration; secondary fungal or bacterial infection can occur if the area remains wet.
Clinical Course: The musty odor and sogginess will resolve within 24 hours of keeping the skin dry. The redness will fade over several days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if the skin forms large blisters, if it begins to drain thick yellow pus, or if the redness spreads.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Sweat became trapped under the tight brace, causing the top layer of skin to become waterlogged and fragile, similar to staying in a bath too long. The odor is from normal skin bacteria thriving in the moisture.
Actionable Steps: Do not put the brace directly on wet skin. Use a cotton barrier sleeve. Wash the brace padding regularly and allow it to dry completely.

SMS INFORMATION

Dx: Skin maceration under brace. No serious infection. Keep skin and brace dry. Use a cotton sleeve under the brace. Watch for spreading redness or pus.

SMS EDUCATION

